.TH KVIO 1 "September 2026" "ebpf-syscall" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kvio \- project, issue, capture, replay, and benchmark KV-cache storage IO
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kvio
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kvio
answers four different questions about a storage-backed KV cache:
what IO a model configuration should produce, what IO a real workload asks
for, what commands reached the NVMe device, and whether a storage tier can
sustain controlled pressure.
These are different claims and use different commands.
.PP
Commands that issue KV IO use LMCache's real raw-block engine.  Commands that
capture device IO use Linux NVMe tracepoints.  A fio bundle preserves a
captured requested stream, while
.B kvio bench
runs a controlled workload and does not claim to replay a capture.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B plan
Project command sizes and counts from model and cache geometry.  This needs no
GPU, application trace, or storage device.  It predicts neither reuse nor
application timing.
.TP
.B trace
Compile timestamped agent requests into a chunk-level cache plan.  Promptful
LMCache traces can derive content identity with a pinned tokenizer.  Promptless
TraceLab records retain observed token and cache accounting but must model
logical chunk identity.
.TP
.B workload
Issue stores and loads through LMCache's real raw-block engine.  It accepts a
model configuration or a plan made by
.BR "kvio trace" .
This command may write to its target.
.TP
.B sweep
Exercise the real engine over a parameter sweep.
.TP
.B replay
Materialize projected KV IO against a real device.  This is distinct from fio
replay of an observed device capture.
.TP
.B validate
Compare a model projection with an engine recording.
.TP
.B record
Run
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
to capture commands at the NVMe driver.  Select one namespace with
.B \-\-disk
when making a replayable capture.
.TP
.B perfetto
Join semantic object records to device commands and write a Perfetto trace.
The offset join uses an object's disjoint byte range and operation time window;
it does not require an engine cookie.
.TP
.B report
Compute SQL metrics from a converted trace.
.TP
.B sem2manifest
Convert semantic JSONL records into a replay manifest.
.TP
.B iolog
Translate an NVMe JSONL capture into a fio version-3 request stream.  With
.B \-\-bundle-dir
it also writes normalized commands, block and NVMe-passthrough job files, a
translation certificate, and checksums.
.TP
.B fio-certify
Use the independent Rust validator to check that a bundle still describes the
captured operations, offsets, lengths, order, and rounded timestamps.
.TP
.B compare
Compare an original device capture with one or more replay captures.  This is
the runtime referee; it checks what fio, Linux, and the NVMe driver actually
issued.  With exactly one replay,
.B \-\-update-bundle
stores the hash-bound result in the matching confidential bundle.
.TP
.B release-build
Build a bounded, unsigned results-only candidate.  Version 1 accepts only
enumerated storage questions, metrics, coarse ratio bands, evidence classes,
and residual-disclosure labels.  It rejects free text, exact numerical results,
paths, source hashes, unknown fields, duplicate keys, and non-draft status.
.TP
.B release-example
Print the canonical version 1 result draft for editing and use with
.BR release-build .
.TP
.B release-verify
Check the candidate's closed grammar, exact two-file inventory, regular-file
types, and payload hash.  Success establishes format conformance only.  It
reports internal evidence and release authorization as not checked and always
reports that export is not allowed.
.TP
.B bench
Run evidence-labeled, sustained fio workloads derived from kvspill.  These
measure restore pressure, small-read interference, eviction pressure, and
storage headroom.  Except for profiles labeled measured, they are controlled
stress shapes rather than recorded production workloads.  The target is
destructive unless protected by the command's safety checks.
.TP
.B bench-compare
Compare medians from repeated benchmark result logs.
.TP
.B doctor
Check the installed engine modules, Python dependencies, Rust validator,
tracer, and fio.
.SH WORKFLOWS
To preserve an observed device-request stream, run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kvio record -> kvio iolog -> kvio fio-certify
            -> run fio while recording -> kvio compare
.fi
.RE
.PP
Static certification proves that the files describe the intended finite
stream.  Only the second capture establishes what the runtime issued.
.PP
To derive IO from real agent requests, run
.B kvio trace
and then
.BR "kvio workload" ,
with
.B kvio record
alongside the workload.
.PP
Use
.B kvio bench
for controlled sustained pressure.  Do not describe its output as replay
fidelity.
.PP
To build and inspect a bounded results-only draft, run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kvio release-example > result.json
kvio release-build result.json candidate
kvio release-verify candidate
.fi
.RE
.PP
The candidate is not an authorization to transfer data.  Connect internal
evidence, human approval, signer trust, and revocation to the organization's
existing controls before it leaves that boundary.
.SH TRACE MINIMIZATION AND PRIVACY
.B nvme_tp_monitor
does not read application payloads.  Its capture contains device and namespace
identity, operation, LBA, length, issue time, queue and command identifiers,
and completion latency.  It contains no prompt, tensor, feature value, graph,
or file content.
.PP
.B kvio iolog
further reduces a replay artifact to operation, exact byte offset, length, and
relative time, plus certificate metadata.  A caller may use a placeholder
filename such as
.I /dev/source
and select the real target with
.B KVIO_TARGET
at replay time.
.PP
This is payload-free and data-minimized; it is not a proof of anonymity.
Offsets reveal locality and address range, timings reveal request cadence, and
device size or an unusual access pattern can identify a workload.  Do not
publish a raw
.BR nvme_uring_cmd_monitor (1)
capture made with
.B \-\-kv
without review: that mode records each 16-byte object key as
.BR key_hex .
kvio does not yet provide a general sanitizer that remaps offsets and time
while stating the fidelity lost by that transform.  This work is tracked in
.IR tools/kvio/TODO.md .
.PP
Keep three boundaries separate.  A capture is confidential source evidence.
The current fio bundle is a confidential fidelity artifact: it preserves exact
placement and timing and contains a digest of the source capture.  It is not an
external release package.  An external release needs a fresh allowlisted
format, independent evidence, and human authorization.  kvio implements a
bounded results-only draft and format checker, but not evidence authentication,
release authorization, or a transformed trace-release format.
.PP
Capture schema version 1 starts with one
.B capture_meta
record, identifies one selected
.B \--disk
scope, and ends with exactly one
.B drops
record.  Replay consumers reject duplicate JSON keys, unknown versions, mixed
device or namespace identities, and records after that footer.  To inspect an
older unversioned capture, pass
.BR \--allow-legacy-capture .
This compatibility option cannot restore missing scope evidence.
.SH DGRAPHFIN EXAMPLE
The DGraphFin case study demonstrates how a payload-free device trace can show
the benefit of an architectural change.  DGraphFin is a public
financial-fraud graph with 3.7 million nodes and 17 floating-point features,
or 68 useful bytes, per node.  It stands in for a private graph so the full
experiment can be reproduced.
.PP
The naive NeighborLoader arm samples scattered neighbors and reads the pages
that contain them.  The page-aware arm reads a page as a unit and consumes
multiple feature vectors from it.  The published 12-second measurements on
the same data and SSD report:
.PP
.RS
.TS
tab(|);
l r r r.
arm|useful bytes|device bytes|commands
NeighborLoader|2.65 MB|1140 MB|240,698
page-aware|58.3 MB|502 MB|61,498
.TE
.RE
.PP
Device bytes divided by useful feature bytes fell from 431 to 8.6, roughly a
50-fold reduction in that read-amplification metric.  Device traffic itself
fell by about 2.3 times, and command count by about 3.9 times.  Keeping these
numbers separate matters because the page-aware arm also makes more useful
progress.
.PP
The application emitted useful-byte markers and the tracer recorded device
commands on the same monotonic clock.  The visualization therefore shows the
gap between application intent and device mechanism without storing feature
values or graph contents.  Exact offsets and timing remain potentially
sensitive metadata.
.SH FIDELITY
.SS Requested stream
.B kvio iolog
rejects an incomplete capture, reported drops, unsupported operations,
duplicate JSON keys or sequence values, unknown capture versions, mixed
device/namespace scope, non-terminal accounting, missing or conflicting LBA
geometry, unaligned ranges, and arithmetic overflow.  It sorts commands by monotonic timestamp
and stable sequence, emits relative fio microseconds, reparses the result, and
certifies the ordered operation, offset, and length stream.  Timestamp
quantization is less than one microsecond.
.PP
.B kvio fio-certify
independently checks the normalized stream, fio iolog, certificate, hashes,
sequence, alignment, range arithmetic, timestamp rounding, and canonical fio
encoding.  This can catch a replay whose syntax is legal but whose target
offset changed.
.SS Executed stream
Run fio while making a second drop-free device capture, then use
.BR "kvio compare" .
Exact device-stream equality requires the same ordered operation, byte offset,
and byte length tuples.  Equal command counts or size histograms alone do not
establish equality.
.SS Time and latency
Issue timing is covered at two levels.  The bundle retains relative issue
times at fio's microsecond resolution.  After replay,
.B kvio compare
rebases each capture to its first command and reports issue-time error at the
50th and 99th percentiles and the maximum.
.PP
.BR nvme_tp_monitor (1)
also records command completion latency.
.B kvio compare
reports completion-latency distributions for the original and replay
captures.  Within each capture it pairs a completion with its command by
hardware queue and NVMe command ID.  For example, queue 2 may reuse command ID
7 after ID 7 completes.  Reuse before completion is ambiguous, so kvio refuses
the per-command latency claim.  Missing or orphan completions and inconsistent
latency timestamps also disable it.  When both captures pair completely and
their command tuples match, kvio reports the per-command latency-error
distribution.  It does not measure application end-to-end latency, model
latency, time to first token, or time per output token.
.PP
Fio pacing, submission engines, Linux scheduling, and the controller can
change timing even when the input file is exact.  Re-recording is therefore
required for a runtime timing claim.
.SS Historical DGraphFin replay
The old DGraphFin replay matched rounded command count, total bytes, and the
request-size distribution.  Its old referee did not compare ordered offsets,
and its exporter compressed time by 1,000 because it treated fio
microseconds as milliseconds.  The tools are fixed; the hardware run must be
repeated before claiming ordered-offset or paced-timing fidelity for that
historical result.
.SH EXAMPLES
Build and install the suite:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make all
make kvio
sudo make install
kvio doctor
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build and install the smaller bank-local surface without LMCache, PyTorch,
models, downloads, or engine-driving commands:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make kvio-offline
sudo make install-kvio-offline
kvio doctor
.fi
.RE
.PP
This remains the same
.B kvio
entry point.  It contains capture, fio translation, independent
certification, device-stream comparison, bounded-result commands, and
.BR doctor .
The verifier build disables Cargo registry access.  The install refuses an
existing package root so files from a full install cannot enter the smaller
boundary.
.PP
Exercise the installed translation path with invented public data and no
device access:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cd /usr/local/libexec/ebpf-syscall/tools/kvio
kvio iolog offline-capture-v1.example.jsonl /dev/fixture \
    --bundle-dir /tmp/kvio-replay
kvio fio-certify /tmp/kvio-replay
kvio compare same:offline-capture-v1.example.jsonl:\
offline-capture-v1.example.jsonl
.fi
.RE
.PP
Project a KV stream without a GPU:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kvio plan --model Llama-3.1-8B --chunk-tokens 256 --op store
.fi
.RE
.PP
Make and check a portable replay bundle:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo kvio record --disk nvme1n1 --dur 60 --jsonl source.jsonl &
source_monitor=$!
# Run the source workload after the monitor reports that it attached.
wait "$source_monitor"
kvio iolog source.jsonl /dev/source --bundle-dir replay
kvio fio-certify replay
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run fio only against a verified disposable target, record that run, and
compare it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo kvio record --disk nvme2n1 --dur 60 --jsonl replay.jsonl &
replay_monitor=$!
sleep 1
sudo KVIO_TARGET=/dev/nvme2n1 fio replay/replay-block.fio
wait "$replay_monitor"
kvio compare original:source.jsonl:replay.jsonl \
    --update-bundle replay
kvio fio-certify replay
.fi
.RE
.PP
The update requires the bundle's source-capture hash and normalized command
stream to match
.IR source.jsonl .
It records the replay hash and runtime verdict, then refreshes
.IR SHA256SUMS .
The bundle retains exact placement and timing and remains confidential.
